2. BSP Kernel and Distros
=========================

The following table summarizes the valid Wind River Linux distros for this BSP.
'Y' in each content cell stands for supported; 'N' stands for not supported:

  +--------------+-------------+------------------+
  | valid/distro |   wrlinux   | wrlinux-std-sato |
  +--------------+-------------+------------------+
  |    valid     |      Y      |         Y        |
  +--------------+-------------+------------------+

For the supported kernel type for this BSP, check the TARGET_SUPPORTED_KTYPES
by running 'bitbake -e | grep "^TARGET_SUPPORTED_KTYPES"'.

Note: The preempt-rt kernel type can be used with this BSP/Machine.

3. Board Specific Patches
=========================

To get a list of patches applied to the kernel specific to this BSP along with
patch descriptions use git-whatchanged on the default kernel. For example:

	%> cd tmp-glibc/work-shared/<bsp_name>/kernel-source
	%> git whatchanged <kernel_version>/standard/base..<kernel_version>/standard/<bsp_name>


4. Boot Instructions
====================

4.1 Device Trees
----------------
Now the ZCU102 board is required to use a device tree to boot Linux.
Two types of device tree files are provided and different files are
suitable for different scenarios.

4.1.1 Kernel Device Trees
-------------------------
The kernel device tree files describe the Versal board hardware features,
except for FPGA implementations. In other words, these files are only used
in scenarios without FPGA implementations.

The device tree files and their target boards are listed below:

	zynqmp-zcu102-rev1.0.dtb

They will be installed to <PROJECT_DIR>/build/tmp/deploy/images/amd-zynqmp/
and the first partition of the WIC image along with the kernel Image.

The device tree source files are located at:
	<PROJECT_DIR>/build/tmp/work-shared/amd-zynqmp/kernel-source/arch/arm64/boot/dts/xilinx

They can be modified and compiled by executing the following commands:
	(build-host)$ cd <PROJECT_DIR>; . ./environment-setup-x86_64-wrlinuxsdk-linux; . ./oe-init-build-env
	(build-host)$ bitbake -c devshell virtual/kernel
	(build-host)$ vim arch/arm64/boot/dts/xilinx/the_file_you_edit*.dts
	(build-host)$ make xilinx/zynqmp-zcu102-rev1.0.dtb

Then the new dtb files can be found at:
	<PROJECT_DIR>/build/tmp/work/amd_zynqmp-wrs-linux/linux-yocto/<kernel_version>/linux-amd_zynqmp-standard-build/arch/arm64/boot/dts/xilinx/

4.1.2 system.dtb
----------------
Different from the kernel device trees above, system.dtb is used for scenarios
with FPGA. It is generated by the device-tree recipe in meta-xilinx. Its
principle is to generate system.dtb based on board dts files downloaded from
https://github.com/Xilinx/device-tree-xlnx.git and Programming Logic dtsi file
pl.dtsi which is extracted from the Xilinx Support Archive(xsa) file.

If "DISTRO_FEATURES:append = ' xilinx-bootloader'" is added to conf/local.conf,
the device-tree recipe will generate system.dtb, install it to
	<PROJECT_DIR>/build/tmp/deploy/images/amd-zynqmp/
and the first partition of the WIC image.

The system.dtb should be used as long as the system involves FPGA implementation.

4.2 Boot Mode
-------------
The ZCU102 boards supports three boot modes: SD card, QSPI32 and JTAG..

4.2.1 SD Boot Mode
------------------
First refer to the section "7.1 BOOT.BIN" to build a bootable WIC
image and burn it to SD card at host-pc:

   (build-host)$ sudo dd if=wrlinux-image-<rootfs_type>-amd-zynqmp.rootfs.wic of=/dev/sdaX

Plug the SD card into the ZCU102 board SD socket and set the DIP
switch SW6 setting like:

   SW6[4]  SW6[3]  SW6[2]  SW6[1]
   OFF     OFF     OFF     ON

Power on the ZCU102 board or press the POR button SW2.

4.2.2 QSPI32 Boot Mode
----------------------
First we boot the board with SD Card by following the steps in the
section "4.2.1 SD Boot Mode". At the U-Boot stage, when the message
"Hit any key to stop autoboot:" appears, hit any key, then run the
following commands to flash the images on the QSPI daughter card:

   (u-boot)> sf probe 0 0 0
   (u-boot)> sf erase 0x0 0x4000000
   (u-boot)> fatls mmc 0:1
   (u-boot)> fatload mmc 0:1 0x70000000 BOOT.bin
   (u-boot)> sf write 0x70000000 0x0 <BOOT.BIN_filesize_in_hex>

Note that the "<BOOT.BIN_filesize_in_hex>" is a hexadecimal value of
the size of BOOT.BIN.

After flashing the images, turn off the power switch on the board,
and change the SW6 boot mode pin settings to QSPI boot mode:

   SW6[4]  SW6[3]  SW6[2]  SW6[1]
   ON      ON      OFF     ON

Power cycle the board. The board now boots up using the BOOT.BIN in
the QSPI flash.

4.2.3 JTAG Boot Mode
--------------------
The JTAG Boot Mode mainly uses xsct on host-pc and it is out of the scope
of this README. Please refer to the document Zynq UltraScale+ Device Technical
Reference Manual(UG1085).

4.3 Boot Linux From U-Boot
--------------------------
This section provides the examples about how to setup u-boot to boot
Linux via network and SD Card.

4.3.1 Network
-------------
First you will need a host machine with the network servers TFTP and NFS
correctly installed and configured.

First find the compressed archive rootfs file at:
	<PROJECT_DIR>/build/tmp/deploy/images/amd-zynqmp/wrlinux-image-<rootfs_type>-amd-zynqmp.rootfs.tar.bz2

Then extract this file to <nfsroot_path>:
   (build-host)$ sudo tar jxf wrlinux-image-<rootfs_type>-amd-zynqmp.rootfs.tar.bz2 -C <nfsroot_path>

And copy the kernel and dtb file to <tftpserver_path>:
   (build-host)$ cp Image <tftpserver_path>
   (build-host)$ cp system.dtb <tftpserver_path>

Set the u-boot environment variables and boot:
   (u-boot)> setenv serverip <tftpserver_ip>; setenv ipaddr <board_ip>
   (u-boot)> setenv bootargs console=ttyPS0,115200 earlycon=cdns,mmio,0xFF000000 clk_ignore_unused root=/dev/nfs rw nfsroot=$serverip:<nfsroot_path> ip=dhcp
   (u-boot)> tftpboot 0x10000000 Image; tftpboot 0x18800000 system.dtb; booti 0x10000000 - 0x18800000

4.3.2 SD Card
-------------
If DISTRO_FEATURES contains "xilinx-bootloader", there is a boot.scr that
contains U-Boot commands to boot Linux from SD Card automatically. There
is no need to reconfigure u-boot.

If DISTRO_FEATURES does not contain "xilinx-bootloader", execute the following
commands to set the u-boot environment variables and boot:
   (u-boot)> setenv bootfile Image; setenv fdtfile zynqmp-zcu102-rev1.0.dtb; setenv loadaddr 0x10000000; setenv fdtaddr 0x18800000;
   (u-boot)> setenv bootargs 'onsole=ttyPS0,115200 root=/dev/mmcblk0p2 rw rootwait earlycon=cdns,mmio,0xFF000000 clk_ignore_unused'
   (u-boot)> setenv bootcmd 'fatload mmc 0:1 $loadaddr $bootfile; fatload mmc 0:1 $fdtaddr $fdtfile; booti $loadaddr - $fdtaddr';
   (u-boot)> saveenv; run bootcmd;

5. WIC Notes
============

User can use the OpenEmbedded Image Creator to create the properly partitioned
image on a SD card. It generates partitioned images from existing OpenEmbedded
build artifacts. Please refer to the following URL for more detailed partition
information about WIC:

https://docs.yoctoproject.org/singleindex.html#creating-partitioned-images-using-wic

5.1 Build a WIC image within prebuilt BOOT.BIN
----------------------------------------------

BOOT.BIN is the bootloader for zcu102 board. It can be downloaded from the website:

https://xilinx-wiki.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/A/pages/18842316/Linux+Prebuilt+Images

and then add one line below to local.conf:

IMAGE_BOOT_FILES:append = " /< path-to-BOOT.BIN >/BOOT.BIN;BOOT.BIN"

After building project, a partitioned WIC image will be created in the deploy folder as follows:

  path_to_your_project/build/tmp-glibc/deploy/images/amd-zynqmp/wrlinux-image-<rootfs_type>-amd-zynqmp.wic

There are two partitions in this WIC images, the first one is to hold the boot
images(including kernel/dtb/BOOT.BIN), the second is the related root file system.

5.2 Build a bootable WIC image
------------------------------
Besides the above method which uses prebuilt BOOT.BIN, WRLinux can build BOOT.BIN and
create a bootable WIC image by following the steps as below:

     $ cd path_of_project
     $ . ./environment-setup-x86_64-wrlinuxsdk-linux
     $ . ./oe-init-build-env
     $ cat << _EOF >> conf/local.conf
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'bootgen'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'u-boot-xlnx'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'u-boot-xlnx-scr'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'xilinx-bootbin'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'fsbl'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'arm-trusted-firmware'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'pmufw'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'device-tree'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx-standalone += 'pmu-firmware'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx-standalone += 'fsbl-firmware'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'u-boot-xlnx-uenv'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'trusted-firmware-a'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx-standalone += 'embeddedsw-source'
DISTRO_FEATURES:append = " xilinx-bootloader"
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:meta-python += 'python3-humanfriendly'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:virtualization-layer += 'lopper'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'python3-anytree'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'u-boot-tools-xlnx'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'binutils'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx-standalone-sdt += 'sdt-artifacts aiefal aienginev2 audio-formatter avbuf axicdma axicdma-example axidma axidma-example axiethernet axiethernet-example axipcie axipmon axipmon-example axis-switch axivdma axivdma-example bram bram-example can can-example canfd canfd-example canps canps-example cframe cfupmc clk-wiz clk-wiz-example clockps clockps-example common coresightps-dcc csi csi2tx csi2txss csudma csudma-example dcsub ddrcpsu devcfg devcfg-example dfeccf dfeccf-example dfeequ dfeequ-example dfemix dfemix-example dfeofdm dfeofdm-example dfeprach dfeprach-example dfxasm dfxasm-example dmaps dmaps-example dp12 dp12rxss dp12txss dp14 dp14rxss dp14txss dp21 dp21rxss dp21txss dpdma dphy dppsu dsi dsi2rx dsirxss dsitxss dual-splitter emaclite emaclite-example emacps emacps-example emc gpio gpio-example gpiops gpiops-example hdcp1x hdcp22-cipher hdcp22-cipher-dp hdcp22-common hdcp22-mmult hdcp22-rng hdcp22-rx hdcp22-rx-dp hdcp22-tx hdcp22-tx-dp hwicap i2srx i2stx i3c i3cpsx i3cpsx-example iic iic-example iicps iicps-example intc intc-example iomodule ipipsu ipipsu-example llfifo llfifo-example mbox mboxpsu-exampmcdma mcdma-example mipicsiss mipi-rx-phy mipi-tx-phy mmidp mutex mutex-example nandps nandpsu nandpsu-example ospipsv ospipsv-example pciepsu pciepsu-example prc prd qspips qspips-example qspipsu qspipsu-example resetps resetps-example rfdc rfdc-example rtcpsu rtcpsu-example scugic scugic-example scutimer scuwdt sd-fec sdiaud sdi-common sdps sdps-example spdif spi spips spips-example sysmon sysmon-example sysmonpsu sysmonpsu-example sysmonpsv sysmonpsv-example tft tmrctr tmrctr-example tmr-inject tmr-manager trafgen trafgen-example trngpsv trngpsx ttcps ttcps-example uartlite uartlite-example uartns550 uartns550-example uartps uartps-example uartpsv uartpsv-example ufspsxc usb usb-example usbps usbpsu usbpsu-example v-axi4s-remap v-csc v-deinterlacer v-demosaic v-demosaict v-gamma-lfrmbuf-rd v-frmbuf-rd-example v-frmbuf-wr v-frmbuf-wr-exa